#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A TEMPLATE. Copy it, do not edit it in place.
#
#   cp app/services/rates/example_parser.rb.template \
#      app/services/rates/bnr_xml_parser.rb
#
# The `.template` extension keeps it out of autoloading; a `.rb` file here that
# defines nothing Rails expects would be loaded and would fail.
#
# Naming is the whole of the wiring. `parser: bnr_xml` in
# db/exchange_rate_sources.yml resolves to Rates::BnrXmlParser by
# convention — there is no third place to register anything, and no list to add
# yourself to. Rename the class to match your key and it is connected.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

module Rates
  # One paragraph on WHOSE feed this is and what the numbers mean — a
  # month-end spot rate, a monthly average, a daily fixing. Someone reading a
  # figure years from now needs to know which.
  #
  # Then paste a REAL EXCERPT of the response, three or four lines of it. Every
  # parser here does, and it is the single most useful thing in the file: it
  # shows the shape without anyone having to fetch the feed to see it.
  #
  #   <DataSet xmlns="https://www.bnr.ro/xsd">
  #     <Body><OrigCurrency>RON</OrigCurrency>
  #       <Cube date="2026-08-21">
  #         <Rate currency="EUR">5.2581</Rate>
  #         <Rate currency="HUF" multiplier="100">1.4467</Rate>
  #
  # Record what is ODD about it, and why your code does what it does. Every
  # parser in this directory has at least one oddity, and every one of them
  # cost real time to find.
  class ExampleParser
    # THE CONTRACT. One class method, three possible answers:
    #
    #   { rates: { "EUR" => 5.2581, ... },   valid_from: Date, valid_to: Date }
    #       Success. `rates` maps a currency code to how many units of it one
    #       unit of the SOURCE'S BASE currency buys — the base being what
    #       `base:` declares in exchange_rate_sources.yml.
    #
    #   [ {...}, {...} ]
    #       MANY periods from one response, same shape each. What a DAILY
    #       source returns: its history arrives as one file holding every
    #       banking day, and fetching a day at a time is not on offer — a
    #       daily feed's ordinary url usually holds today alone. See
    #       Rates::EcbDailyXmlParser.
    #
    #   nil
    #       REFUSE. The response was not usable: wrong shape, no rates, or —
    #       importantly — it does not say what period it covers.
    #
    #   :no_data_yet
    #       The response was fine and simply does not reach that period. Not a
    #       failure: a month-average source is empty until its month ends, and
    #       treating that as an error emails somebody every night for a week.
    #
    # `requested_date` is what the caller asked for. Use it to CHOOSE among
    # what the feed returned — never to label what it returned.
    def self.parse(body, requested_date: nil)
      # ── 1. Parse defensively ────────────────────────────────────────────
      # Nokogiri::XML(body) then `doc.remove_namespaces!` if the feed declares
      # one, or CSV.parse(body, headers: true). Return nil, do not raise, if it
      # is not the document you expected.

      # ── 2. Take the PERIOD FROM THE FEED ────────────────────────────────
      # ⚠️ The rule that has cost the most here. Asking HMRC for July on
      # 1 August once stored August's rates labelled July, and every sterling
      # figure for that month was quietly wrong — right numbers, wrong month,
      # no error.
      #
      # If the feed declares its own span (HMRC's CSV has Start date and End
      # date), read both. If it declares a month, derive the span from that. If
      # it declares nothing at all, return nil rather than assuming the caller
      # was right.
      #
      # A DAILY feed returns a one-day span: valid_from == valid_to. That is
      # supported — the lookup asks "which span contains this date" and never
      # cares how long the span is. Declare `frequency: daily` on the source.

      # ── 3. NORMALISE, so nothing downstream learns your feed is unusual ──
      # Whatever your central bank does strangely stops HERE. Two real cases,
      # and a new feed is likely to have one of them:
      #
      #   · PER-100 QUOTES. ESTV publishes "100 DKK" costing 12.4792 francs and
      #     BNR marks HUF with multiplier="100". Divide. Miss it and every
      #     figure in that currency is out by a factor of a hundred while
      #     looking entirely plausible.
      #
      #   · INVERTED DIRECTION. Some feeds quote "what one unit of the foreign
      #     currency costs in ours", which is the reverse of what is stored.
      #     ESTV needs `multiplier / kurs`. Work out which way round yours is
      #     by checking a rate you know: if 1 EUR is about 5 RON, then a
      #     RON-based feed showing 5.2581 for EUR is quoting the inverse.
      #
      # Also: skip non-positive rates, and dedupe if rows repeat a currency —
      # HMRC's file has one row per COUNTRY, so every dollar-using country
      # repeats the same rate.

      # ── 4. Return ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      # { rates: rates, valid_from: from, valid_to: to }
      raise NotImplementedError
    end
  end
end

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BEFORE YOU BELIEVE IT WORKS
#
# ⚠️ HIT THE LIVE FEED. A passing unit test is not evidence. HMRC's parser had a
# green test and matched nothing against the real response, because Net::HTTP
# returns ASCII-8BIT whatever the charset header says, and the header
# `Currency Units per £1` arrived as raw bytes. The test used a Ruby string.
#
#   bin/rails runner 'body = Net::HTTP.get(URI("<your url>")); \
#                     pp Rates::YourParser.parse(body, requested_date: Date.current)'
#
# Then check three things a fixture cannot tell you:
#   · a rate you can verify independently is right, and the right way up
#   · the span matches the period you actually asked for
#   · a period the feed does NOT have returns :no_data_yet or nil, not a rate
#     for some other period
#
# Month padding differs between publishers and is a 404 either way: HMRC wants
# 2026-8, the Bundesbank 2026-06. The url template offers %{month} and %{month2}.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
